A project import brings a whole project across from Manuskript or from Plume Creator: its
chapters, its cast, its notes. If what you have is a folder of documents instead, see
[importing documents](:help-import-documents).

An import creates a **new** Skribisto project.

## Starting an import

With a project open, choose *Work ▸ Import from*, then *Manuskript (.msk)…* or *Plume Creator
(.plume)…*. In the Launcher, the same importers sit behind *Create from…*.

The panel asks for:

- the project to read;
- a destination folder;
- a file name for the `.skrib` it will create.

Nothing is written until you press *Import*. The import shows its progress, and *Cancel* leaves
nothing behind.

## Pointing at a Manuskript project

Manuskript saves a project in either of these shapes, and both are accepted:

- **Folder mode**, the usual one: a tiny `.msk` file plus a folder of the same name beside it.
  The folder holds the project and the `.msk` just points at it, so keep the two together.
- **Single-file mode**: the whole project sits inside the `.msk`.

Use *Choose file…* for a `.msk` and *Choose folder…* for the folder; in folder mode either one
works. The import tells you which copy it read.

## What Manuskript brings with it

- Your text, your notes and your word goals.
- The "compile" tick, which becomes *Include in exports*. An item switched off stays off, with
  everything inside it.
- An item's summaries, joined into its one synopsis: the one-sentence summary first, then the
  fuller one.
- Your cast, your world entries and your plots, as notes in a *Story bible* binder beside the
  manuscript, one group each. A scene's point of view points at the character it names; plots
  keep their steps and their links.
- Your labels, as coloured tags.
- Your status list, as the project's own statuses, under your names.
- Your revision history: open a scene, and its earlier versions are in the *Versions* panel.

In your prose, Manuskript stored a marker for a character, a world entry, a plot or another
item. Those markers are put back as the words they stood for, with a link to what they named.

## What has no home here

A few Manuskript things have no matching field in Skribisto:

- **Per-item icons.**
- **The colour beside a character's name.** Skribisto colours tags instead.
- **The nested summaries of the whole book** (situation, sentence, paragraph, page, full). The
  longest becomes the book's synopsis, and they are kept together in a *Summary* note in the
  *Story bible* binder.
- **Details with no field of their own**, such as the series and the volume. They land beside
  it, in a *Project information* note.

## How parts and chapters are worked out

Skribisto reads the depth of the whole outline and picks one ladder for all of it:

- If no folder holds another folder, folders become chapters.
- If a folder anywhere holds another, the outermost folders become parts, and the folders
  inside them chapters.

Everything is wrapped in one Book, which the writing plan, the analysis and the export scopes
are built around.

It is a starting point: any item can be retyped afterwards from the binder. See
[the writing model](:help-writing-model) for what each type is.

## Coming from Plume Creator

Choose a `.plume` or `.plume_backup` file, from any version of Plume.

- Books, acts, chapters and scenes arrive with their text, their synopses and their notes.
- A scene separator becomes a scene break in the prose above it.
- Plume's groups of characters and places become folders of notes, each group with its own tag.
  An entry's other names arrive as its *Also known as* list.
- The entries a scene featured become links on that scene.
- Plume's revision stages become the project's statuses.
- Your personal dictionary comes too.

Items in Plume's rubbish bin are **not** migrated. Empty the bin, or restore what you want to
keep, before importing. Plume carries no author name, so set yours in *Settings ▸ Work:
<project> ▸ Author*.

## After the import

A message offers *Open now*, which opens the new project.

The import's notes come in their own message, in plain language behind a *Details* button. It
names what could not be carried across exactly.

The new project is a single `.skrib` file. If you kept your old one as a folder for version
control, use *Work ▸ Save as folder…* afterwards. See
[backups and versions](:help-backups-and-versions).
